{{liushu|p}} – a horse with its head facing the left, showing a flowing mane in the wind. In the bronze inscriptions, the head was often simplified into an eye ({{m-lite|zh|sc=Hani|目}}). The legs eventually evolved into four dots ({{m-lite|zh|sc=Hani|灬}}, unrelated to {{m-lite|zh|sc=Hani|火}}).

Contrast with {{m-lite|zh|sc=Hani|鹿|t=deer}}, which saw a very different development, and {{m-lite|zh|sc=Hani|𢊁//|tr=-}} (as in {{m-lite|zh|sc=Hani|薦}}), which is a hybrid: it has the legs of {{lang|zh|馬}} ({{m-lite|zh|sc=Hani|灬}}) but the head of {{m-lite|zh|sc=Hani|鹿}}.

===Etymology===
{{zh-forms|s=马|alt=𫠉,傌,𢒗,𢒠,𢒧,𩡬,𩡮,抹-Sichuan senses,碼-“to bully”}}
{{zh-wp|zh|yue|gan|hak:mâ|cdo:mā|nan:bé|wuu:马}}

“Horse” – from {{inh|zh|sit-pro|*k-m-raŋ ~ s-raŋ}}. The sense of “big” is derived from “horse”; compare the English uses of {{m|en|horse}}.

For the insect prefix sense, see {{m-lite|zh|sc=Hani|螞}}. It has converged with the sense of “big”.

The surname is popularly known to be prevalent among [[Hui]] Muslims, where it is likely derived from {{der|zh|ar|مُحَمَّد|t=Muhammad}}, although the surname {{lang|zh|馬}} itself predates Islam.

